WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.920
An Easter egg over 40 years in the making&nbsp;
— "Indiana Jones and the Dial of Destiny"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.540
slipped in one reference that made fans&nbsp;
hurt almost everywhere. Spoilers ahead!

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.080
In a conclusion as epic as “The Dial&nbsp;
of Destiny,” it was inevitable that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.340
Dr. Jones's last great adventure would be&nbsp;
filled with callbacks. But director James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.480
Mangold saves the best hat tip for the&nbsp;
end when we get a reunion between Indy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.440
and a love he’d thought he’d lost. Well,&nbsp;
there’s no time like the present, eh, Indy?

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.240
After returning from a trip of over two thousand&nbsp;
years in the past, Indy wakes from his near-fatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.460
gunshot wound back in his apartment with no idea&nbsp;
of how he got there. Turns out, after a swift hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.320
to the face from his darling goddaughter, Helena,&nbsp;
they, along with Teddy and a German pilot we never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.120
see again, escaped the past, where Indy survived&nbsp;
his injuries thanks to the miracle of modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.740
medicine. There’s still one surprise left for the&nbsp;
old time traveler, though. Minutes after waking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:54.720
up, he comes face to face with his estranged&nbsp;
wife, Marion, played as always by Karen Allen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.040
When the two are left to hash it out, it isn’t&nbsp;
long before they kiss and make up exactly how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.980
did 42 years earlier. For franchise fans, it’ll no&nbsp;
doubt get you in the feels here…and here…and here.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.060
Proving that they still have chemistry even&nbsp;
after all these years, Marion and Indy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.920
moment is the perfect send-off the franchise&nbsp;
needed, taking things full circle and showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.000
that even through grief, these two were meant&nbsp;
for each other. Having both mourned the loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.360
of their son Mutt in different ways, Marion&nbsp;
reveals she still hurts from what’s happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.140
and the fact they’ve been distant from one&nbsp;
another. Indy asks her where it doesn’t hurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.120
and he plants a kiss everywhere she points. It’s&nbsp;
a heartfelt role reversal from a moment that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.800
just a staple in the franchise but a true piece&nbsp;
of cinema history in “Raiders of the Lost Ark.”

00:01:35.112 --> 00:01:37.212
During the first adventure of Indiana Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.212 --> 00:01:40.800
Marion is tending to Indy’s wounds.&nbsp;
After being asked where it doesn’t hurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.360
they finally show affection for each other,&nbsp;
with Marion kissing Indy before he drifts off.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.560
"Jones. Jooones."

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.800
It’s easily one of their best moments together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.460
so seeing it replicated in "Dial&nbsp;
of Destiny" hits just right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.840
rounding things off with a loving reunion&nbsp;
and an almost heartfelt goodbye to the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.580
adventurer. He got a happy ending he deserved,&nbsp;
but it’s one that finally sees him tending to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.000
emotional injuries rather than the physical&nbsp;
ones we’ve seen him take after so many years.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.860
While the closing moment of “The Dial of&nbsp;
Destiny” is a great little nod to Indy’s origins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.540
it also ties everything in a charming bow&nbsp;
as our worn-down hero gets the resolution he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.980
needs. For most of Indy’s last great adventure,&nbsp;
there’s an added bit of regret to the standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.000
stubbornness we’ve always come to anticipate&nbsp;
with our beloved explorer. This isn’t just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.140
story about a hero past his prime, but&nbsp;
the regret of past mistakes he fears he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.460
can never resolve. He even goes as far as to&nbsp;
accept that, quite literally, staying in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.780
past is the best course of action as opposed to&nbsp;
a present that he believes has no place for him.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.920
Of course, that’s not the case. While he’s&nbsp;
helpless to change the loss of his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.300
who died after joining the army against his&nbsp;
father’s wishes, he can still fix some things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.860
“The Dial of Destiny” gives Indy the chance to&nbsp;
be a hero in a different way and return to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.120
the loving husband to Marion that she’s needed,&nbsp;
even if he didn’t originally have it in him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.280
be that particular hero. The world will always&nbsp;
need Indiana Jones, but Marion needs him more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.067
and that’s the most important thing. After&nbsp;
all, as Marion said from the very beginning…

00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:09.767
"I'm your g—damn partner."

